cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or manuscript, please cite it as below."
title: "Evaporation Toward a Degenerate Horizon: Islands, Asymptotics and the Horizonless Branch of the Hayward Black Hole"
abstract: >-
  We study the late-stage evaporation of a Hayward-type regular black hole whose regularization scale
  is tied to the mass so that the core density is mass-independent, eps^3 = 2GM l^2/c^2. The metric
  has a critical mass M* = (3 sqrt(3)/4) l c^2/G at which its inner and outer horizons merge. Far from
  M* it reproduces Schwarzschild to fractional order (l/M)^2, so to a distant observer the object is
  an extremely time-dilated collapsed star. We make four points. First, the quasi-static (eternal
  black hole) approximation used in the island literature is justified along the evaporation: an
  adiabaticity parameter is of order 10^-83 for a 10 solar-mass hole, is small at M*, and, with the
  near-extremal mass-loss law, vanishes as the endpoint is approached. Second, within the continuous
  semiclassical mass-loss law the transition at M* is never completed in finite time. The horizon area
  at M* is a fixed nonzero constant while the surface gravity vanishes, so the emission power scales
  as eps^2 in the mass excess eps = M - M* and eps ~ 1/u. This is the analogue for this metric of the
  unattainability of extremality known for charged black holes, and infinite evaporation times are
  known for regular black holes in general; it is not a new structural statement. The continuous law
  fails in a small window above M*, where the emission of a single quantum or near-extremal quantum-
  gravity effects become important. Third, the island mechanism is argued, at leading adiabatic order
  and through a heuristic extension of the static prescription, to remain intact along the approach.
  The entropy plateau approaches, at leading order, the area value 3 pi l^2/G, the leading term of the
  extremal Hayward island entropy of Kim and Nam (2021); the extremal result is not the limit of the
  non-extremal one, and we do not resolve the subleading terms. Fourth, for M < M* the geometry has no
  horizon and supports no island, by one argument in two steps: a purity argument reduces the
  extremization to the area term, and the area then grows linearly near the point of maximal redshift,
  leaving no critical point. For M >~ 0.81 M* it also has a stable inner light ring, as a general
  theorem requires for smooth ultracompact objects satisfying the null energy condition; a test-scalar
  WKB calculation, cross-validated against a time-domain evolution, finds genuinely long-lived trapped
  modes there, consistent with the expectation that the horizonless configuration is not a stable
  endpoint. Whether general relativity coupled to standard matter can supply the core is treated in a
  companion paper.
